# Implementation Plan - Complete EcoGuardian AI

EcoGuardian AI is a full-stack personal carbon footprint management platform. The application frontend is fully built and compiles successfully, and the Python ML service starts successfully on port 8000. To complete the remaining work and ensure a seamless developer/evaluation experience, we will add support for automatic database fallback to an in-memory MongoDB server, auto-seed the database on startup, and improve placeholder handling for the AI assistant.

## Proposed Changes

### Database & Seeding Component

To make the application zero-setup and run out-of-the-box (even if a local MongoDB service is not running on port 27017), we will integrate `mongodb-memory-server` as a fallback.

#### [NEW] [seedHelper.js](file:///c:/Users/Asus/sem%203%20mini/backend/scripts/seedHelper.js)
- Extract the seeding logic from `seed.js` into a reusable module.
- Check and seed default admin user, demo user, and weekly challenges only if they do not already exist.

#### [MODIFY] [seed.js](file:///c:/Users/Asus/sem%203%20mini/backend/scripts/seed.js)
- Refactor to reuse the new `seedHelper.js` logic.

#### [MODIFY] [db.js](file:///c:/Users/Asus/sem%203%20mini/backend/config/db.js)
- Attempt connection to local MongoDB (`mongodb://localhost:27017/ecoguardian`).
- If connection fails or times out (configured for 5 seconds), automatically start an in-memory MongoDB server using `mongodb-memory-server` and connect mongoose to it.

#### [MODIFY] [server.js](file:///c:/Users/Asus/sem%203%20mini/backend/server.js)
- Import `seedHelper.js` and call it asynchronously after `connectDB()` resolves, ensuring that default users and challenges are always seeded on server startup.

